**Test the empty-order error**

No unit test covers an empty order. Assert the required message through the public function:
```js
test('rejects orders with no items', () => {
  assert.throws(() => applyDiscount({ items: [] }), { message: 'empty order' });
});
```

<details><summary>Why this was flagged</summary>

kind: missing_test, confidence: high
</details>

<!-- specguard:fp=e767c1ab545b681a4c5721f92a25224e4c471db9 -->

**Test both sides of the discount threshold**

No test pins the inclusive 100 boundary. Assert literal results on both sides:
```js
test('applies the large-order discount at 100', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 100, qty: 1 }] }), 95);
});
test('does not apply the large-order discount below 100', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 99, qty: 1 }] }), 99);
});
```

<details><summary>Why this was flagged</summary>

kind: missing_test, confidence: high
</details>

<!-- specguard:fp=148f76fd8e575cd2d79cc12fe46f7b1d81f5c076 -->

**Test trimming a non-empty name**

The whitespace-only rejection test does not verify the returned value for a non-empty name. Pin the trimmed result:
```js
test('trims surrounding whitespace from names', () => {
  assert.equal(normalizeName('  Ada  '), 'Ada');
});
```

<details><summary>Why this was flagged</summary>

kind: missing_test, confidence: high
</details>

<!-- specguard:fp=f9cd82f1ced8bc2cbff9f0e46c78120a69e0d16c -->

**Test both sides of the discount threshold**

No assertion checks the 100 boundary. Add cases just below and at the threshold:
```js
test('does not discount totals below 100', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 99.99, qty: 1 }] }), 99.99);
});

test('discounts totals of 100 or more', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 100, qty: 1 }] }), 95);
});
```

<details><summary>Why this was flagged</summary>

kind: missing_test, confidence: high
</details>

<!-- specguard:fp=4d37a3f67e2fabc8d5dfcfdfa2127f583ddd3160 -->

**Test store-credit subtraction**

No unit assertion supplies store credit and checks the resulting total. Add a direct example:
```js
test('subtracts store credit from the total', () => {
  assert.equal(
    applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 80, qty: 1 }], credit: 20 }),
    60,
  );
});
```

<details><summary>Why this was flagged</summary>

kind: missing_test, confidence: high
</details>

